  "text": "התחיל\n\nהתחיל\n\n Start by considering the given conditions. First, the area of each triangle $A_iA_1A_{i+1}$ is 1. Second, the cosine of the angle $\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}$ is $\\frac{12}{13}$. Third, the perimeter of the polygon is 20. We need to find the length of $A_1A_2 + A_1A_{11}$ in the form $\\frac{m\\sqrt{n} - p}{q}$, and then sum $m$, $n$, $p$, and $q$.\n\nWe can use the cosine rule to find the lengths of the sides of the triangles $A_iA_1A_{i+1}$. The cosine rule is given by:\n\\[c^2 = a^2 + b^2 - 2ab\\cos(\\theta)\\]\n\nIn our case, $a = A_1A_i$, $b = A_iA_{i+1}$, and $c = A_1A_{i+1}$. We know that $a = b = 1$ and $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{12}{13}$. Therefore, we have:\n\\[c^2 = 1^2 + 1^2 - 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\frac{12}{13} = 1 + 1 - \\frac{24}{13} = 2 - \\frac{24}{13} = \\frac{26}{13} - \\frac{24}{13} = \\frac{2}{13}\\]\n\\[c = \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{13}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{13}\\]\n\nSo, each side of the triangle is $\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{13}$.\n\nNext, we need to find the lengths of $A_1A_2$ and $A_1A_{11}$. We can use the fact that the perimeter of the polygon is 20. Let $A_1A_2 = a_1$ and $A_1A_{11} = a_{11}$. The perimeter is the sum of all the sides, so we have:\n\\[a_1 + a_{11} + 10 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{13} = 20\\]\n\\[a_1 + a_{11} = 20 - 10 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{13} = 20 - \\frac{10\\sqrt{26}}{13} = \\frac{260 - 10\\sqrt{26}}{13}\\]\n\nWe need to express this in the form $\\frac{m\\sqrt{n} - p}{q}$. Here, $m = 260$, $n = 26$, $p = 10$, and $q = 13$. Therefore, $m + n + p + q = 260 + 26 + 10 + 13 = 299$.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{299}\\]",
